{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>A staff.</b> The qof with pataḥ, on account of attachment (dibbuq), like מעשר בקר וצאן (tithe of cattle and sheep), מספד תמרורים." "<b>White, moist.</b> לבנה — its wood does not appear white except when it is moist. But the לוז and the chestnut, their wood is white whether moist or dry; therefore it says לח with regard to לבנה." "<b>And luz.</b> This is almonds, as we say in Bekhorot: a chick [is considered ready] for twenty-one days, and correspondingly luz on a tree. And this is not 'coldara,' for indeed there is [a period] from its setting [of fruit] until its picking of more than twenty-one days. And furthermore: nuts and almonds are translated in the Yerushalmi: משח דבוטנים ומשח דלוזין (oil of nuts and oil of almonds)." "<b>And he peeled.</b> It has no [exact] parallel; it is a term of splitting in the language of the Talmud. And he made of them a booth and a enclosure to bring the flock into them. And the females could not flee from the males, and they would conceive immediately, and this is what is written: והיה הקשורים ליעקב (and the ones tied would be Jacob's)."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The point appears to be that the qamatz-qamatz form מַקֵּל shifts to qamatz-pataḥ מַקַּל in construct/attachment to the following word; the examples cited are other construct-state nouns with the same vowel pattern."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:54.028236066Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}